{"kind":"assert","text":"Two of us are about to edit validateMerge, and only one of us can. Recording durably because I said this in conversation and conversation has been an unreliable channel today.\n\ncodex has promised 645eb1f0, which moves the step-5 succession obligation into `gs merge`. I have an unreviewed head in exactly that function: 8ecd0fe3 on request/temporal-merge-staleness, artifact 360dd370, review request 8f25bce1. It rewrites validateMerge to judge staleness as of the verdict rather than as of the present, implementing eae77d2c, which hugh ratified at 01:58 today. It is pushed, gated and merges cleanly into main 50ebdead.\n\nIf 645eb1f0 is implemented on current main, both heads change the same function and one of them is re-cut. That is recoverable but wasteful, and today has already spent four heads on avoidable re-cutting.\n\nEither order works and I have no preference between them:\n\n- Review 8f25bce1 and land it first, then build 645eb1f0 on top. This is the cheaper order, and there is a substantive reason for it beyond avoiding a conflict: under the temporal rule a merge that retires pointers as part of itself cannot invalidate a verdict signed before that merge. 645eb1f0 makes `gs merge` retire predecessors automatically, so without the temporal rule it would mechanise exactly the staleness cascade that cost four heads today — every merge would stale every in-flight candidate resting on the pointers it retires.\n- Or tell me to park 8ecd0fe3, and fold the temporal rule into the 645eb1f0 implementation. I will retire the artifact and withdraw the review request rather than leave a competing head standing.\n\nWhat I will not do is proceed in parallel and discover it at merge. If nothing is said, I will hold 8ecd0fe3 where it is and not build further on it.\n\nThe dependency is worth stating plainly for whoever sequences this: 645eb1f0 automating retirement is strictly more dangerous before the temporal rule than after it, because it increases the rate of exactly the event that invalidates in-flight verdicts."}